  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">School Emergency and Disaster Preparedness in Port-au-Prince and surrounding areas</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">School children were amongst one of the group most affected by the earthquake that hit Haiti on 12th January 2010. 85% of schools in Port-au-Prince areas have been severely damaged or destroyed and a lot of children died in the catastrophe. In April 2010, the Haitian government reopened the schools but the situation remains critical as most of them are located in high risk areas and the conditions in which they are operating are even worse than before the earthquake.
The goal of the project is to reduce school children vulnerability to natural disasters by contributing towards the implementation of a disaster risk reduction programme in the most affected areas of Port-au-Prince. The purpose is to make schools in those areas safer, enabling them to act as a locus for disaster risk reduction, institutionalizing the implementation of emergency and disaster preparedness strategy within the Haitian education system.
The project will work in selected schools managed by the diocesan office for education (BDE) and the Education Support Centre (CAP). An effective methodology will be developed by CAP that can be replicated in other schools, influencing national level policy and practice in ways that can be easily replicated in other areas. A distinctive approach, adapting participatory vulnerability analysis for use in schools will be a defining feature of the project, helping to build the awareness and analysis of children, parents, teachers, ministry of education.
The primary beneficiaries will be 55 schools in Port-au-Prince and surrounding areas with over 28,000 pupils (and their parents) and over 385 teachers (and school management committee members / directors). 
This project will then increase the level of awareness of risk in schools, building knowledge among teachers, parents and children. Information on hazards, mitigation measures, hygiene, first aid and preparedness plans will be shared, both in the classroom and out through the parent committees.  Overall children, teachers, parents will be trained on how to protect themselves against natural disasters like floods, earthquakes, landslides, etc.</narrative>
  </description>
